[0] Knowing the lens's radius of curvature $R$ lets us calculate the radii of the dark Newton rings.
Light of wavelength $\lambda$ cancels at certain places, making those places dark.
[1] The air gap between the lens and the flat glass plate has the same thickness all around each circle centered on their contact point.
That is why the dark places form rings.
    break: {"before":"Knowing the lens's radius of curvature $R$ lets us calculate the radii of the dark Newton rings.\nLight of wavelength $\\lambda$ cancels at certain places, making those places dark.","after":"The air gap between the lens and the flat glass plate has the same thickness all around each circle centered on their contact point.\nThat is why the dark places form rings."}
[2] For the dark ring with order $k$, call its radius $r_k$ and the air gap's thickness there $h_k$.
The right triangle in Figure 200 connects these quantities.
    break: {"before":"The air gap between the lens and the flat glass plate has the same thickness all around each circle centered on their contact point.\nThat is why the dark places form rings.","after":"For the dark ring with order $k$, call its radius $r_k$ and the air gap's thickness there $h_k$.\nThe right triangle in Figure 200 connects these quantities."}
[3] The Pythagorean theorem gives:
$R^2=r_k^2+(R-h_k)^2$.
Expanding gives $2Rh_k=r_k^2+h_k^2$.
    break: {"before":"For the dark ring with order $k$, call its radius $r_k$ and the air gap's thickness there $h_k$.\nThe right triangle in Figure 200 connects these quantities.","after":"The Pythagorean theorem gives:\n$R^2=r_k^2+(R-h_k)^2$.\nExpanding gives $2Rh_k=r_k^2+h_k^2$."}
[4] The lens's radius of curvature is much larger than the wavelength and $h_k$.
The gap is very thin, so $h_k\ll r_k$.
    break: {"before":"The Pythagorean theorem gives:\n$R^2=r_k^2+(R-h_k)^2$.\nExpanding gives $2Rh_k=r_k^2+h_k^2$.","after":"The lens's radius of curvature is much larger than the wavelength and $h_k$.\nThe gap is very thin, so $h_k\\ll r_k$."}
[5] The squared thickness is tiny compared with the squared ring radius, so we neglect it and get $2Rh_k\approx r_k^2$.
The approximate thickness is:
$h_k\approx\frac{r_k^2}{2R}$ (7.6).
    break: {"before":"The lens's radius of curvature is much larger than the wavelength and $h_k$.\nThe gap is very thin, so $h_k\\ll r_k$.","after":"The squared thickness is tiny compared with the squared ring radius, so we neglect it and get $2Rh_k\\approx r_k^2$.\nThe approximate thickness is:\n$h_k\\approx\\frac{r_k^2}{2R}$ (7.6)."}
[6] Compare the light reflected from the opposite sides of the thin air gap.
The wave reflected from the glass plate below travels an extra distance $2h_k$ through the air, going down and back up.
    break: {"before":"The squared thickness is tiny compared with the squared ring radius, so we neglect it and get $2Rh_k\\approx r_k^2$.\nThe approximate thickness is:\n$h_k\\approx\\frac{r_k^2}{2R}$ (7.6).","after":"Compare the light reflected from the opposite sides of the thin air gap.\nThe wave reflected from the glass plate below travels an extra distance $2h_k$ through the air, going down and back up."}
[7] When it reflects from air onto glass, its phase also changes by $\pi$: its oscillation is reversed.
The wave reflected at the lens's underside meets the air boundary from inside the glass.
    break: {"before":"Compare the light reflected from the opposite sides of the thin air gap.\nThe wave reflected from the glass plate below travels an extra distance $2h_k$ through the air, going down and back up.","after":"When it reflects from air onto glass, its phase also changes by $\\pi$: its oscillation is reversed.\nThe wave reflected at the lens's underside meets the air boundary from inside the glass."}
[8] It does not have the same reflection reversal there.
The lower reflection's phase change is equivalent to an extra path difference $\frac{\lambda}{2}$, not extra distance actually traveled.
    break: {"before":"When it reflects from air onto glass, its phase also changes by $\\pi$: its oscillation is reversed.\nThe wave reflected at the lens's underside meets the air boundary from inside the glass.","after":"It does not have the same reflection reversal there.\nThe lower reflection's phase change is equivalent to an extra path difference $\\frac{\\lambda}{2}$, not extra distance actually traveled."}
[9] This explains the source footnote's warning: travel through the gap alone does not give the full path difference used for interference.
Here the wavelength is the wavelength in the air gap, approximated as the vacuum wavelength as in the source calculation.
    break: {"before":"It does not have the same reflection reversal there.\nThe lower reflection's phase change is equivalent to an extra path difference $\\frac{\\lambda}{2}$, not extra distance actually traveled.","after":"This explains the source footnote's warning: travel through the gap alone does not give the full path difference used for interference.\nHere the wavelength is the wavelength in the air gap, approximated as the vacuum wavelength as in the source calculation."}
[10] A dark ring depends on the phase difference from both travel and reflection.
Using the interference-minimum condition from Section 46:
    break: {"before":"This explains the source footnote's warning: travel through the gap alone does not give the full path difference used for interference.\nHere the wavelength is the wavelength in the air gap, approximated as the vacuum wavelength as in the source calculation.","after":"A dark ring depends on the phase difference from both travel and reflection.\nUsing the interference-minimum condition from Section 46:"}
[11] $2h_k+\frac{\lambda}{2}=\frac{\lambda}{2}(2k+1)$,
where $k=0,1,2\cdots\cdots$.
    break: {"before":"A dark ring depends on the phase difference from both travel and reflection.\nUsing the interference-minimum condition from Section 46:","after":"$2h_k+\\frac{\\lambda}{2}=\\frac{\\lambda}{2}(2k+1)$,\nwhere $k=0,1,2\\cdots\\cdots$."}
[12] Substitute the expression for the air gap's thickness $h_k$ from (7.6) into the dark-ring condition.
The wavelength $\lambda$ and the lens's radius of curvature $R$ then determine the dark ring's radius $r_k$:
    break: {"before":"$2h_k+\\frac{\\lambda}{2}=\\frac{\\lambda}{2}(2k+1)$,\nwhere $k=0,1,2\\cdots\\cdots$.","after":"Substitute the expression for the air gap's thickness $h_k$ from (7.6) into the dark-ring condition.\nThe wavelength $\\lambda$ and the lens's radius of curvature $R$ then determine the dark ring's radius $r_k$:"}
[13] $r_k=\sqrt{k\lambda R}$ (7.7).
This relation describes the reflected Newton rings above: a very thin gap, nearly perpendicular light, and the same reflection-phase relationship.
    break: {"before":"Substitute the expression for the air gap's thickness $h_k$ from (7.6) into the dark-ring condition.\nThe wavelength $\\lambda$ and the lens's radius of curvature $R$ then determine the dark ring's radius $r_k$:","after":"$r_k=\\sqrt{k\\lambda R}$ (7.7).\nThis relation describes the reflected Newton rings above: a very thin gap, nearly perpendicular light, and the same reflection-phase relationship."}
[14] Measure a dark ring's radius and identify its order.
If you also know the lens's radius of curvature, the relation just found lets you determine the wavelength.
    break: {"before":"$r_k=\\sqrt{k\\lambda R}$ (7.7).\nThis relation describes the reflected Newton rings above: a very thin gap, nearly perpendicular light, and the same reflection-phase relationship.","after":"Measure a dark ring's radius and identify its order.\nIf you also know the lens's radius of curvature, the relation just found lets you determine the wavelength."}
[15] The source reports $\lambda_{\text{红}}=8\times10^{-5}$ centimeters for red light and $\lambda_{\text{紫}}=4\times10^{-5}$ centimeters for violet light.
It uses these values to describe the wavelengths of the other spectral colors as lying between them (see color plate V; source citation: 彩图 V).
    break: {"before":"Measure a dark ring's radius and identify its order.\nIf you also know the lens's radius of curvature, the relation just found lets you determine the wavelength.","after":"The source reports $\\lambda_{\\text{红}}=8\\times10^{-5}$ centimeters for red light and $\\lambda_{\\text{紫}}=4\\times10^{-5}$ centimeters for violet light.\nIt uses these values to describe the wavelengths of the other spectral colors as lying between them (see color plate V; source citation: 彩图 V)."}
[16] These are the source's reported values, not precise boundaries defining red and violet.
All visible-light wavelengths are very short.
    break: {"before":"The source reports $\\lambda_{\\text{红}}=8\\times10^{-5}$ centimeters for red light and $\\lambda_{\\text{紫}}=4\\times10^{-5}$ centimeters for violet light.\nIt uses these values to describe the wavelengths of the other spectral colors as lying between them (see color plate V; source citation: 彩图 V).","after":"These are the source's reported values, not precise boundaries defining red and violet.\nAll visible-light wavelengths are very short."}
[17] The source compares them with sea waves whose average wavelength is a few meters.
Imagine enlarging such a sea wave until it stretches across the Atlantic from the American coast to the European coast.
Enlarge a light wave by the same factor, and its wavelength would still be only a little wider than a page of this book.
    break: {"before":"These are the source's reported values, not precise boundaries defining red and violet.\nAll visible-light wavelengths are very short.","after":"The source compares them with sea waves whose average wavelength is a few meters.\nImagine enlarging such a sea wave until it stretches across the Atlantic from the American coast to the European coast.\nEnlarge a light wave by the same factor, and its wavelength would still be only a little wider than a page of this book."}
[18] Interference shows that light behaves as a wave and gives us a way to measure its wavelength.
Frequency distinguishes the pitch of a sound.
    break: {"before":"The source compares them with sea waves whose average wavelength is a few meters.\nImagine enlarging such a sea wave until it stretches across the Atlantic from the American coast to the European coast.\nEnlarge a light wave by the same factor, and its wavelength would still be only a little wider than a page of this book.","after":"Interference shows that light behaves as a wave and gives us a way to measure its wavelength.\nFrequency distinguishes the pitch of a sound."}
[19] The color of monochromatic light is also related to frequency;
within the same medium, wavelength can distinguish its color.
    break: {"before":"Interference shows that light behaves as a wave and gives us a way to measure its wavelength.\nFrequency distinguishes the pitch of a sound.","after":"The color of monochromatic light is also related to frequency;\nwithin the same medium, wavelength can distinguish its color."}
[20] In the source's relation $\lambda\nu=c$, wavelength times frequency gives the speed of light in vacuum.
The wavelength in this relation is therefore the vacuum wavelength.
After changing the medium, we must not substitute its wavelength while still using the vacuum speed.
    break: {"before":"The color of monochromatic light is also related to frequency;\nwithin the same medium, wavelength can distinguish its color.","after":"In the source's relation $\\lambda\\nu=c$, wavelength times frequency gives the speed of light in vacuum.\nThe wavelength in this relation is therefore the vacuum wavelength.\nAfter changing the medium, we must not substitute its wavelength while still using the vacuum speed."}
[21] Color is a sensation produced when our eyes receive light;
light itself has different wavelengths.
    break: {"before":"In the source's relation $\\lambda\\nu=c$, wavelength times frequency gives the speed of light in vacuum.\nThe wavelength in this relation is therefore the vacuum wavelength.\nAfter changing the medium, we must not substitute its wavelength while still using the vacuum speed.","after":"Color is a sensation produced when our eyes receive light;\nlight itself has different wavelengths."}
[22] The source gives about $10^{-6}$ centimeters as a small wavelength difference the eye can distinguish.
That is the source's estimate, not the same limit for every color and viewing condition.
    break: {"before":"Color is a sensation produced when our eyes receive light;\nlight itself has different wavelengths.","after":"The source gives about $10^{-6}$ centimeters as a small wavelength difference the eye can distinguish.\nThat is the source's estimate, not the same limit for every color and viewing condition."}
[23] The source also claims that most animals see only black and white.
That is not an accurate general rule: animals differ in their ability to distinguish colors.
    break: {"before":"The source gives about $10^{-6}$ centimeters as a small wavelength difference the eye can distinguish.\nThat is the source's estimate, not the same limit for every color and viewing condition.","after":"The source also claims that most animals see only black and white.\nThat is not an accurate general rule: animals differ in their ability to distinguish colors."}
[24] Light's wavelength changes when it enters another medium.
Fill the gap between the lens and glass plate with water or a transparent liquid of refractive index $n$ instead of air.
    break: {"before":"The source also claims that most animals see only black and white.\nThat is not an accurate general rule: animals differ in their ability to distinguish colors.","after":"Light's wavelength changes when it enters another medium.\nFill the gap between the lens and glass plate with water or a transparent liquid of refractive index $n$ instead of air."}
[25] For such liquids that keep the reflection-phase relationship described above, the interference rings become smaller.
Keep the light source and lens unchanged when comparing the rings, so the change shows the medium's effect.
    break: {"before":"Light's wavelength changes when it enters another medium.\nFill the gap between the lens and glass plate with water or a transparent liquid of refractive index $n$ instead of air.","after":"For such liquids that keep the reflection-phase relationship described above, the interference rings become smaller.\nKeep the light source and lens unchanged when comparing the rings, so the change shows the medium's effect."}
[26] Why do the rings shrink after adding the liquid?
When light enters the transparent medium considered here from vacuum, refractive index $n$ describes the factor by which its speed decreases.
    break: {"before":"For such liquids that keep the reflection-phase relationship described above, the interference rings become smaller.\nKeep the light source and lens unchanged when comparing the rings, so the change shows the medium's effect.","after":"Why do the rings shrink after adding the liquid?\nWhen light enters the transparent medium considered here from vacuum, refractive index $n$ describes the factor by which its speed decreases."}
[27] Speed, wavelength, and frequency in the medium still satisfy $v=\lambda\nu$.
The frequency stays unchanged, so the wavelength becomes $\frac{1}{n}$ of its vacuum value.
    break: {"before":"Why do the rings shrink after adding the liquid?\nWhen light enters the transparent medium considered here from vacuum, refractive index $n$ describes the factor by which its speed decreases.","after":"Speed, wavelength, and frequency in the medium still satisfy $v=\\lambda\\nu$.\nThe frequency stays unchanged, so the wavelength becomes $\\frac{1}{n}$ of its vacuum value."}
[28] The ring-radius relation must therefore use the wavelength in the gap's medium, not the vacuum wavelength.
The source's statement that wavelength changes by a factor of $n$ means that it becomes shorter, not longer.
    break: {"before":"Speed, wavelength, and frequency in the medium still satisfy $v=\\lambda\\nu$.\nThe frequency stays unchanged, so the wavelength becomes $\\frac{1}{n}$ of its vacuum value.","after":"The ring-radius relation must therefore use the wavelength in the gap's medium, not the vacuum wavelength.\nThe source's statement that wavelength changes by a factor of $n$ means that it becomes shorter, not longer."}
[29] The frequency stays unchanged, so entering another medium alone does not change the light into another color.
With the reflection-phase relationship unchanged, a shorter wavelength gives smaller dark rings.
